<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Turgesh Khaganate</h1><p class="st-meta">699 CE – 766 CE (approx.) · capital Suyab</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkic khaganate of the Chu and Ili valleys that led the steppe resistance to the Arab conquest of Transoxiana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Turgesh were one of the tribes of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">On Oq</a> confederation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>. Their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuzhile">Wuzhile</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a> around 699 as the Western Turkic state collapsed, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqal">Saqal</a> succeeded him before being killed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-turkic-khaganate">Second Turkic Khaganate</a> in 711.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suluk">Suluk</a>, who rose around 716, the Turgesh became a major power, allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a> and the Sogdian princes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. Arab sources call him Abu Muzahim, 'the one who jostles'. He routed an Arab army on the 'Day of Thirst' in 724, inflicted heavy losses on another at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-defile">Battle of the Defile</a> in 731, and sustained a court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Talas</a> on Sogdian trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suluk">Suluk</a> was murdered in 738 after a defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kharistan">Kharistan</a>, and civil war between the Yellow and Black Turgesh factions followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> intervened, and by 766 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a> and the Turgesh lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turgesh-khaganate.jpg" alt="Turgesh Khaganate" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: Szegedi László · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T%C3%BCrgesh_Khaganate,_borders.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>699 CE – 766 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suyab</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wuzhile, Saqal, Suluk</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turgesh, Turgish, Tuergesh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=732&amp;focus=turgesh-khaganate" aria-label="Turgesh Khaganate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6500 657 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turgesh Khaganate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6820 913L6820 913M6820 913L6820 913"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6820 913h0M6820 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6820 913h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6820 913h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Suyab. <a href="/atlas?year=732&amp;focus=turgesh-khaganate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
